# DigiSwasthya Product Definition Document (PDD)

# Part 4 — Business Relationships & Business Rules

---

# Purpose

This section defines how every business entity interacts with every other business entity.

It establishes the rules that govern the platform.

These rules apply regardless of user interface, platform, or implementation.

Every workflow, feature, screen, and business decision must follow these rules.

If the application allows behaviour that violates these rules, the application is incorrect.

---

# Core Philosophy

DigiSwasthya models real pharmaceutical business operations.

The platform should never allow software convenience to violate real business logic.

Business accuracy always takes priority over automation.

---

# Business Relationship Model

The platform contains three active participants.

```
Platform Administrator

        │

        ▼

Verifies Businesses

        │

 ┌──────┴──────┐

 ▼             ▼

Stockist ◄────► Pharmacy

        Trades
```

The administrator governs.

The stockist supplies.

The pharmacy purchases.

No user may perform another user's responsibilities.

---

# Relationship 1

## Business → Users

A business may have multiple users.

Every user belongs to exactly one business.

A user cannot belong to multiple businesses simultaneously.

Removing a user must never delete business records.

Business records always belong to the business, not the individual user.

---

# Relationship 2

## Pharmacy ↔ Stockist

A pharmacy may trade with multiple stockists.

A stockist may trade with multiple pharmacies.

Trading requires an approved business relationship.

Without an approved relationship:

- products cannot be ordered
- pricing cannot be accessed
- invoices cannot be created
- payments cannot be made

---

# Relationship 3

## Stockist → Product Catalogue

Every stockist owns exactly one catalogue.

The catalogue contains products available for sale.

Products become visible only to eligible pharmacies according to future visibility rules.

A stockist may:

- add products
- remove products
- modify pricing
- update availability

Removing a product from the catalogue must never delete historical transactions.

---

# Relationship 4

## Product → Inventory

Every inventory record belongs to one product.

Inventory represents physical stock.

Inventory changes only through recognised inventory movements.

Inventory cannot change arbitrarily.

---

# Relationship 5

## Inventory → Batch

Inventory exists at the batch level.

Every stock movement references a batch.

Different batches of the same product remain independent.

Expiry dates belong to batches, not products.

---

# Relationship 6

## Pharmacy → Order

A pharmacy creates purchase orders.

Orders are requests.

Orders do not guarantee fulfilment.

A pharmacy may:

- create
- edit (while permitted)
- cancel (while permitted)

A pharmacy cannot approve its own order.

---

# Relationship 7

## Stockist → Order

The stockist receives purchase requests.

The stockist decides whether to fulfil the request.

The stockist may:

- accept
- reject
- partially fulfil (future capability)
- dispatch
- complete delivery

The stockist owns the fulfilment process.

---

# Relationship 8

## Order → Invoice

Invoices originate from orders.

Invoices cannot exist independently of a completed order fulfilment process unless explicitly supported by future business rules.

One order may generate one or more invoices if future partial fulfilment is supported.

Every invoice always references its originating order.

---

# Relationship 9

## Invoice → Payment

Payments settle invoices.

Payments never modify invoices.

Invoices remain permanent financial records.

Payments only reduce outstanding balances.

Invoices determine financial liability.

Payments determine settlement.

---

# Relationship 10

## Delivery → Order

Deliveries transport ordered goods.

Delivery status affects order status.

Delivery does not automatically indicate payment.

Payment remains a separate business process.

---

# Relationship 11

## Return → Delivered Goods

Returns may only reference delivered products.

Undelivered products cannot be returned.

Returned quantities cannot exceed delivered quantities.

Every returned item references its original order.

---

# Relationship 12

## Return → Credit Note

Approved returns generate credit notes.

Rejected returns generate no financial adjustment.

Credit notes represent financial value.

Returned goods represent physical value.

These are separate concepts.

---

# Relationship 13

## Credit Note → Invoice

Credit notes reduce future invoice liabilities.

Credit notes never modify historical invoices.

Credit notes remain independently traceable financial documents.

---

# Relationship 14

## Notifications

Notifications communicate events.

Notifications never create business events.

Deleting a notification must never affect business data.

---

# Relationship 15

## Messages

Messages assist communication.

Messages never replace business transactions.

Business decisions must always occur through official workflows.

A message saying

"Approved"

does not approve an order.

The order workflow approves the order.

---

# Universal Business Rules

The following rules apply throughout DigiSwasthya.

---

## Rule 1

Every business transaction must have an owner.

---

## Rule 2

Every financial record must be traceable.

---

## Rule 3

Business records must never disappear because of user deletion.

---

## Rule 4

Deleting a business must never destroy historical financial data.

---

## Rule 5

Inventory should never become negative.

---

## Rule 6

Expired inventory should never be supplied.

---

## Rule 7

Every order must reference:

- one pharmacy
- one stockist

---

## Rule 8

Every invoice belongs to an order.

---

## Rule 9

Every payment belongs to one or more invoices.

---

## Rule 10

Every return belongs to delivered goods.

---

## Rule 11

Every credit note belongs to an approved adjustment.

---

## Rule 12

Every notification references a business event.

---

## Rule 13

Every message references participants.

---

## Rule 14

Historical records should never lose integrity.

Business history must always remain reconstructable.

---

## Rule 15

Business actions should always leave an audit trail.

The platform should always know:

- who performed the action
- when it occurred
- what changed

---

# Platform Invariants

The following statements must always remain true.

A pharmacy cannot purchase from itself.

A stockist cannot purchase from itself.

A business cannot approve its own verification.

Users cannot belong to multiple businesses simultaneously.

Invoices cannot exist without a valid business relationship.

Payments cannot exist without financial liability.

Returns cannot exceed delivered quantities.

Inventory cannot become negative.

Expired batches cannot be delivered.

Completed financial records cannot disappear.

Business history cannot become inconsistent.

---

# Future Rules

Future product features may introduce additional business relationships.

Examples include:

- multiple warehouses
- multiple delivery partners
- manufacturer relationships
- inter-stockist transfers
- procurement chains

These future relationships must extend this model rather than replace it.

The integrity of the existing business relationships must always be preserved.